# Paths are relative to the build context (the parent of both repos).
#
# The name is load-bearing. Docker reads `.dockerignore` only from the root of
# the context, which here is a directory no repo owns; BuildKit also reads
# `<Dockerfile>.dockerignore` beside the Dockerfile, and that is what this is.
# It used to be `docker/.dockerignore`, which no build ever read: the host's
# node_modules was copied over the one `npm ci` had just built for Alpine, and
# once the lockfile moved to Vite 8 the bundle step ran the host's stale Vite 6
# and died looking for a musl binary the host never installs.

**/node_modules
**/venv
**/.git
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.coverage

# Built in stage 1 — a stale local copy must never win over it.
noHarm/dist
noHarm/test-results
noHarm/playwright-report
noHarm/android
noHarm/ios

# Secrets stay out of the image. Config arrives as environment variables; a
# .secrets.toml baked into a layer is readable by anyone who pulls the image.
**/.secrets.toml
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Service-account keys. Stage 1 does `COPY noHarm/ ./`, so a key left in that
# repo lands in the front-end build layer. The final stage only takes
# /build/dist, so it never reaches the pushed image — but a build layer is not
# the place to find out.
**/*firebase-adminsdk*.json
**/*service-account*.json
